yoloctl local patch 0003: restore the workload calls OpenShell v0.0.72 allowed.

OpenShell 0.1.6's child hardening filter (openshell-sandbox
src/linux/child_seccomp.rs, prepare) newly denies these calls, which the
v0.0.72 workload filter (openshell-supervisor-process
src/sandbox/linux/seccomp.rs) never denied. That broke GNU make
(posix_spawn with POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS calls setgid/setuid(own id)), nice,
ionice, killpg and process-group test suites inside every box. Restored, as
in v0.0.72:

- unconditionally allowed again: capset, setuid, setgid, setreuid, setregid,
  setresuid, setresgid, setfsuid, setfsgid, setgroups, setpriority,
  ioprio_set
- allowed again on any process except the trusted sandbox itself:
  prlimit64, sched_setaffinity, sched_setattr, sched_setparam,
  sched_setscheduler
- allowed again with PID zero or a negative PID (process groups, killpg),
  except a signal to the sandbox's own process group (-sandbox TGID):
  kill, rt_sigqueueinfo

The sandbox-targeted denies stay because the sandbox's own startup probe
(openshell-sandbox src/main.rs, prlimit sandbox / process-group signal)
requires them; they also protect the trusted sandbox from the workload.
The workload has no capabilities and runs with no_new_privs, so the kernel
still refuses any real identity or capability gain. Every deny v0.0.72 also
had is kept, and the other 0.1.6 denies (signals to the sandbox's own TGID,
F_SETOWN, sethostname, ...) are unchanged.

